# construction/standing-equipment.kyri -- the standing guards, as a list a program can read.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. The roster of standing equipment lived in one paragraph of
# construction/ITINERARY.md, in prose. A paragraph can be read and it can be trusted; it cannot
# be run, counted, or dated. Two consecutive rounds paid for that: REDS %147 found eight
# standing witnesses each holding a count its source had moved past, and REDS %149 found
# the living-pin bound guard unrun for four laps while construction/REDS.md sat 2,050 bytes over
# the bound it measures. Both were caught by a hand that happened to run something.
#
# The tree already seats the strand this file's sibling completes. The grain says a guard
# that cannot red guards nothing (REDS row 59), and every guard here can red. The strand
# beside it, learned the hard way twice: a guard that is never run guards nothing either.
#
# WHAT A ROW MEANS. One `guard` record per standing check, each naming the path a runner
# invokes, the tier it runs on, and the stamp the guard was seated.
#
# WHAT A TIER MEANS. Guards cost wildly different amounts of time. A choir -- one witness that
# sings a whole family of rungs -- takes minutes: `caravan_suite` runs 111 rungs in 8m31s,
# `crypto_suite` runs 74 in 9m06s, and `ales_suite` runs 240 in 233s and 303s over two clean
# passes, all measured on this pier on 20260825, against a whole roster of 20m20s with one of them
# seated. A lap reads the roster twice, cold at the open and hot after `git add`, so every choir on
# one tier would cost most of an hour a lap. So a guard names its own clock:
#
# COST DECIDES THE TIER, AND SO DOES WHAT IS MOVING. `caravan_suite` costs more than `ales_suite`
# and stands on the every-lap tier, because Caravan is the arc under active work and had just held
# a live refusal for two laps. `ales_suite` is cheaper and stands on `cadence`, because Chapter C's
# Lotus rungs are stable. Revisit a row's tier when its family reopens.
#
#   tier lap       every roster run. What a record naming no tier means, which is why none of the
#                  rows below had to be edited when the field arrived.
#   tier cadence   the cadence lap -- the fifth round, where the council rota
#                  closes its cycle -- and any lap where a hand asks for the guard by name.
#                  (The seed once shipped on this clock too; that cadence was CUT 20260826,
#                  and custody gate %1 holds every refresh at Keatons hand.)
#
# A tier is a CADENCE rather than an exemption. REDS %219 was a choir standing off this roster
# altogether, which is a refusal nobody receives; a cadence guard is still heard, on a slower
# clock, and the run card records which clock heard it. A tier word the runner does not know is
# refused at zero, because a guard seated on one would run on no lap at all, in silence. The roster is the authority for what stands;
# construction/standing-equipment-runs.kyri records when each last ran ON THIS PIER, and that card
# is deliberately untracked, so a fresh clone reads `never run here` rather than inheriting
# another machine's memory.
#
# HOW IT IS KEPT HONEST. tools/s/standing_equipment_witness.rish proves every path exists,
# refuses a run card naming a guard the roster never seated, and refuses any recorded RED.
# Add a guard by adding a record here; the meter counts the file rather than a sentence.

format standing-equipment-v1
stamp 20260825.183336
voice Kyri
runner tools/fixtures/s/standing_equipment_run.sh
card construction/standing-equipment-runs.kyri

